.TH file "" "Command"
.SH NAME
file \- guess type of a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBfile \fIname ...\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
file -- guess type of a file
Usage:	file name ...
.HE
For each given
.IR name ,
.B file
prints its best guess as to the type of the file, based on the
file's mode and, for an ordinary file, the first part of its
contents.
If
.I name
cannot be examined (for example, because it does not exist or the
caller lacks permission),
.B file
reports it as not accessible instead.
.PP
For a file that is not an ordinary file,
.B file
reports the mode's category directly, one of
.BR directory ,
.BR "SCCS directory"
(a directory whose name begins with
.BR s. ),
.BR "block special file" ,
.BR "character special file" ,
.BR "block multiplexor file" ,
.BR "character multiplexor file" ,
or
.BR "named pipe" ,
tagging the two special-file cases with their major and minor
device numbers.
.PP
For an ordinary file,
.B file
reads the start of the file and reports one of the following:
.IP \fBempty\fR 0.6i
The file has zero length.
.IP "object file classification"
The file begins with the magic number of a load-and-go object
header.
.B file
goes on to describe whether it is a kernel, a driver, or an ordinary
executable, whether it is 32-bit, a shared library, a library
reference, shared, separate I&D, and whether it still carries
relocation information, followed by the machine type in
parentheses.
.IP \fBarchive\fR
The file begins with the current archive magic number, made by
.BR ar ,
or with one of the older UNIX archive magic numbers, in which case
.B file
reports
.B "seventh edition archive"
or
.B "sixth edition archive"
instead.
.IP "\fBbinary data\fR"
The file contains a byte that is not printable ASCII, white space,
or backspace.
.IP \fBcommands\fR
The file's execute permission bit is set, but it is not an object
file; presumably a shell script.
.PP
Otherwise
.B file
inspects the text for a few recognizable patterns:
a line beginning
.B .globl
means
.BR assembler ;
a line beginning with a period means
.BR "nroff, tbl or eqn input" ;
a line ending in a colon means
.BR commands ;
a line beginning
.B %
followed immediately by
.BR % ,
.BR { ,
or
.B }
means
.BR "yacc or lex input" ;
a mixture of braces and either semicolons or
.B #
lines means
.BR "C program" ;
short, fairly regular line lengths suggest
.BR "xmail encoded text" .
Failing all of these,
.B file
reports
.BR "probably text" .
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ar ,
.B ls
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B file
prints
.B "name: not accessible"
for a
.I name
it cannot
.BR stat (),
and exits with a nonzero status if any
.I name
could not be examined.
.SH NOTES
This is a heuristic guess based on a handful of simple patterns; it
looks only at the first part of the file and can be fooled.
